            "output": "<path>/home/brian/mule_dev/R/entire-agent-zed/README.md</path>\n<type>file</type>\n<content>\n1: # entire-agent-zed\n2: \n3: An external agent plugin for the [Entire.io CLI](https://github.com/entireio/cli) that provides transcript extraction for the [Zed Editor](https://zed.dev) AI Assistant.\n4: \n5: ## How it works\n6: \n7: Zed stores all of its AI chat threads in a local SQLite database, compressing the payloads using `zstd`.\n8: \n9: This agent operates as a **Database Tailer**:\n10: 1. When called, it locates the Zed database.\n11: 2. It queries the database for the most recently modified chat thread matching the current `ENTIRE_REPO_ROOT` environment variable.\n12: 3. It decompresses the `zstd` blob and parses the internal Zed JSON format (`User`, `Agent`, `ToolUse`).\n13: 4. It transforms the chat into the **OpenCode Transcript Format** for efficient checkpoint storage.\n14: \n15: ## Transcript Format\n16: \n17: The transcript is now stored in **OpenCode format**, which is significantly more efficient than the previous Zed SQLite database snapshots:\n18: \n19: ```json\n20: {\n21:   \"info\": {\n22:     \"id\": \"session-id\",\n23:     \"title\": \"Thread title\",\n24:     \"version\": \"0.3.0\",\n25:     \"model\": { \"providerID\": \"google\", \"modelID\": \"gemini-3.1-pro-preview\" }\n26:   },\n27:   \"messages\": [\n28:     {\n29:       \"info\": { \"role\": \"user\", \"id\": \"msg-...\" },\n30:       \"parts\": [\n31:         { \"type\": \"text\", \"text\": \"Hello\" },\n32:         { \"type\": \"tool_use\", \"tool_use\": { \"name\": \"terminal\", \"input\": {...} } }\n33:       ]\n34:     }\n35:   ]\n36: }\n37: ```\n38: \n39: ### Delta Tracking\n40: \n41: The agent tracks message count watermarks between turns to enable **delta-only transcripts**:\n42: \n43: - **turn-start**: Stores current message count as watermark in `.git/entire-sessions/<session-id>.state.json`\n44: - **turn-end**: Loads watermark, returns only new messages since last turn-start\n45: \n46: This dramatically reduces checkpoint sizes. Instead of storing ~10 MB per checkpoint (full SQLite database), we now store only the new messages (~1-50 KB per turn).\n47: \n48: ### Database Locations\n49: \n50: | OS    | Path |\n51: |-------|------|\n52: | Linux | `~/.local/share/zed/threads/threads.db` |\n53: | macOS | `~/Library/Application Support/Zed/threads/threads.db` |\n54: \n55: ## Capabilities\n56: \n57: - `transcript_analyzer`: Extracts and parses Zed AI threads\n58: - `transcript_preparer`: Maps Zed messages to Entire transcript schema\n59: - `hooks`: Git hook lifecycle management (post-commit)\n60: - `token_calculator`: Parses token usage from Zed's `request_token_usage` field\n61: - `secret_redactor`: Masks sensitive keys (API keys, tokens, etc.) before storage\n62: \n63: ## Subcommands\n64: \n65: | Subcommand | Description |\n66: |---|---|\n67: | `info` | Returns agent metadata and capabilities |\n68: | `transcript` | Extracts the latest Zed thread as Entire transcript JSON |\n69: | `parse-hook <event>` | Translates hook events into Entire lifecycle events |\n70: | `install-hooks` | Installs a `post-commit` git hook for automatic lifecycle tracking |\n71: | `uninstall-hooks` | Removes the hook (handles both legacy pre-commit and current post-commit) |\n72: | `are-hooks-installed` | Checks if the hook is installed |\n73: | `calculate-tokens` | Sums token usage from the latest thread |\n74: | `attach` | Captures a research/planning thread that doesn't produce commits |\n75: | `end-session` | Manually ends the current Zed session from the command line |\n76: | `extract-branch` | Correlates Zed transcripts with the branch commit history |\n77: | `get-session-dir` | Returns the directory containing Zed's threads.db |\n78: | `resolve-session-file` | Returns the full path to threads.db |\n79: \n80: ## Hook Lifecycle (post-commit)\n81: \n82: The agent installs a **post-commit** hook (not pre-commit) that fires lifecycle events after each commit:\n83: \n84: ```\n85: [First commit only]\n86:   session-start  →  Initializes the Entire session\n87:   turn-start     →  Starts the first work unit\n88: \n89: [Every commit]\n90:   turn-end       →  Ends the current turn, capturing the transcript delta\n91:   turn-start     →  Starts a new turn for the next work unit\n92: ```\n93: \n94: This design ensures:\n95: - The commit SHA exists when turn-end fires, enabling correlation\n96: - Each \"turn\" represents the conversation between two consecutive commits\n97: - Session ID is derived from the actual Zed thread ID (not a static string)\n98: - The actual last user prompt is extracted from the thread (not a placeholder)\n99: - A message count watermark is included for computing transcript deltas\n100: \n101: ## Attaching Research/Planning Threads\n102: \n103: For Zed threads that don't produce any commits (research, planning, architecture discussions), use the `attach` subcommand:\n104: \n105: ```bash\n106: entire-agent-zed attach\n107: ```\n108: \n109: This fires the full lifecycle (session-start → turn-start → turn-end → session-end) in a single invocation, capturing the entire thread as one turn.\n110: \n111: ## Ending a Session Manually\n112: \n113: To end the current Zed session from the command line:\n114: \n115: ```bash\n116: entire-agent-zed end-session\n117: ```\n118: \n119: This fires `turn-end` → `session-end`, writes a session snapshot for cross-agent handoff, and prints a JSON confirmation. No stdin required.\n120: \n121: ## Cross-Agent Session Handoff\n122: \n123: On every `turn-end`, `session-end`, `attach`, and `end-session`, the agent writes a session snapshot to `.git/entire-sessions/`:\n124: \n125: - **`<session-id>.json`** — Session metadata (`session_id`, `agent_type`, `phase`, `transcript_path`, `last_prompt`, etc.)\n126: - **`<session-id>.transcript.json`** — The full transcript in Entire message format\n127: \n128: This enables the [session-handoff skill](https://github.com/entireio/skills) to pick up Zed sessions from other agents (Claude Code, Codex, Cursor, etc.) without any special configuration.\n129: \n130: ## Branch Timeline Extraction\n131: \n132: The `extract-branch` subcommand correlates all Zed transcripts for a branch with its commit history:\n133: \n134: ```bash\n135: # JSON output (default)\n136: entire-agent-zed extract-branch\n137: \n138: # Markdown report\n139: entire-agent-zed extract-branch --format markdown\n140: \n141: # Specific branch against a base\n142: entire-agent-zed extract-branch --branch feature-x --base develop\n143: ```\n144: \n145: ### What it produces\n146: \n147: The output is a timeline that reconstructs how AI-assisted development progressed across the branch:\n148: \n149: - **Timeline entries** — one per commit, each showing which Zed threads were active around that commit (matched by a 30-minute time window)\n150: - **Thread summaries** — a deduplicated list of all threads with message counts and token usage, reported once per thread (not duplicated across commits)\n151: - **Token totals** — branch-level rollup of input, output, cache read tokens, and API call count across all threads\n152: - **Orphan threads** — research/planning threads that were active for this repo but don't correlate with any commit (useful for capturing work that didn't result in code changes)\n153: \n154: ### Reconstructing thread history from a branch\n155: \n156: To reconstruct the full AI conversation history for a feature branch:\n157: \n158: ```bash\n159: # Step 1: Get the full timeline as JSON\n160: entire-agent-zed extract-branch --branch feature-x --base main > timeline.json\n161: \n162: # Step 2: Get a human-readable summary\n163: entire-agent-zed extract-branch --branch feature-x --base main --format markdown > timeline.md\n164: ```\n165: \n166: The JSON output has this structure:\n167: \n168: ```json\n169: {\n170:   \"branch\": \"feature-x\",\n171:   \"base\": \"main\",\n172:   \"merge_base\": \"abc123...\",\n173:   \"commit_count\": 5,\n174:   \"thread_count\": 2,\n175:   \"token_totals\": {\n176:     \"input_tokens\": 50000,\n177:     \"output_tokens\": 12000,\n178:     \"cache_read_tokens\": 30000,\n179:     \"api_call_count\": 45\n180:   },\n181:   \"thread_summaries\": [\n182:     {\n183:       \"thread_id\": \"uuid-1\",\n184:       \"summary\": \"Implement auth module\",\n185:       \"message_count\": 42,\n186:       \"tokens\": { \"input_tokens\": 30000, \"output_tokens\": 8000, \"...\" : \"...\" }\n187:     }\n188:   ],\n189:   \"timeline\": [\n190:     {\n191:       \"commit\": { \"sha\": \"def456\", \"timestamp\": \"...\", \"author\": \"...\", \"message\": \"feat: add login\" },\n192:       \"files_changed\": [\"src/auth.go\"],\n193:       \"diff_stats\": \"1 file changed, 50 insertions(+)\",\n194:       \"threads\": [\n195:         { \"thread_id\": \"uuid-1\", \"summary\": \"Implement auth module\", \"message_count\": 42, \"transcript\": [...] }\n196:       ]\n197:     }\n198:   ],\n199:   \"orphan_threads\": [\n200:     { \"thread_id\": \"uuid-2\", \"summary\": \"Research OAuth providers\", \"message_count\": 15, \"transcript\": [...] }\n201:   ]\n202: }\n203: ```\n204: \n205: ### Understanding token reporting\n206: \n207: Token counts come from Zed's `request_token_usage` field in each thread's stored data. These are **cumulative totals for the entire thread**, not per-commit deltas. This is because:\n208: \n209: - Zed stores the thread as a single blob — there are no per-message timestamps or per-turn token breakpoints in the database\n210: - The same thread may be matched to multiple commits in the timeline (if the user was working in the same thread across several commits)\n211: \n212: To avoid misleading duplication, tokens are reported at two levels:\n213: \n214: | Level | What it shows |\n215: |-------|---------------|\n216: | `token_totals` | Sum across all unique threads for the branch |\n217: | `thread_summaries[].tokens` | Per-thread cumulative totals (each thread counted once) |\n218: \n219: Per-commit token deltas are **not** available retroactively. However, if the post-commit hooks are running, each `turn-end` event includes a token snapshot. The `entire` CLI can compute deltas from consecutive turn-end events in its own logs.\n220: \n221: ### Tips for analysis\n222: \n223: - **Pipe JSON to `jq`** for ad-hoc queries: `entire-agent-zed extract-branch | jq '.timeline[] | {msg: .commit.message, threads: (.threads | length)}'`\n224: - **Count AI-assisted vs. manual commits**: commits with zero matched threads were likely manual\n225: - **Find the most expensive threads**: `jq '.thread_summaries | sort_by(-.tokens.input_tokens) | .[0]'`\n226: - **Identify orphan research**: orphan threads reveal planning/investigation work that didn't directly produce code\n227: \n228: ## Security & Privacy\n229: \n230: The agent automatically redacts sensitive information from transcripts before they are included in Entire checkpoints:\n231: \n232: - **Key Masking**: Sensitive keys (e.g., `api_key`, `private_key`, `token`, `password`, `auth`, `credential`) are partially masked with `*`, preserving only the first 4 characters for debugging.\n233: - **Type Redaction**: Non-string sensitive values (e.g., objects or booleans assigned to sensitive keys) are replaced with `[REDACTED]`.\n234: - **Output Truncation**: Tool result outputs are truncated to 100 characters to prevent accidental leakage of large sensitive data blobs.\n235: - **Automatic Enforcement**: Redaction is applied during every `turn-end` and `checkpoint` event, ensuring that secrets never leave your local environment in a plain-text format.\n236: \n237: ## Upstream Entire.io CLI Compatibility\n238: \n239: This agent currently exposes a limitation in `entire` v0.5.5 where external agent auto-discovery (`$PATH` scanning for `entire-agent-*` binaries) is filtered out for passive GUI agents. The recommended workaround is to call this binary directly from hooks or use the `install-hooks` subcommand.\n240: \n241: ## Building and Installing\n242: \n243: ```bash\n244: make test      # Run all tests (57 tests)\n245: make install   # Build and install to ~/.local/bin/entire-agent-zed\n246: make uninstall # Remove from ~/.local/bin\n247: make clean     # Remove local build artifact\n248: ```\n249: \n250: The standard workflow after making changes:\n251: \n252: ```bash\n253: make test && make install\n254: ```\n255: \n256: ## Changelog\n257: \n258: ### 2026-04-19 - Secret Redaction\n259: \n260: - Added automatic secret redaction for tool inputs and outputs (`redactTranscript()`)\n261: - Added masking for sensitive keys like `api_key`, `private_key`, `token`, etc.\n262: - Added output truncation for large tool results\n263: - Added tests for redaction logic in `main_test.go`\n264: \n265: ### 2026-04-19 - Delta Transcript Storage\n266: \n267: - Added OpenCode transcript format conversion (`convertToOpenCodeFormat()`)\n268: - Added delta tracking with message count watermarks (`extractDeltaTranscript()`)\n269: - Added session state storage (`.git/entire-sessions/<session-id>.state.json`)\n270: - Reduced checkpoint sizes from ~10 MB to ~1-50 KB per turn\n271: - Added 9 new tests for format conversion and delta extraction\n\n(End of file - total 271 lines)\n</content>",
