ry, I think the right approach is:\n\n1. Diagnose the root cause (CLI-side: `agent add` and `agent list` don't discover external agents).\n2. Fix the CLI source (in /tmp/opencode/entireio-cli), rebuild, and reinstall.\n3. Also fix the zed agent's `detect` subcommand to return `{\"present\": true}` so it shows up as \"available\" (detected).\n4. Verify the git hook fires correctly.\n5. Write a runbook.\n\nBut wait — should I really rebuild the entire CLI? That's a huge undertaking and the user might not want that. Let me reconsider.\n\nActually, let me re-read the user's message once more: \"in the current (0.9.0) version of entire.io cli, installed after running the entire-agent-zed `make install` command\". \n\nHmm, \"installed after running the entire-agent-zed make install command\" — this means the entire CLI was installed (by some process) after running `make install` in the entire-agent-zed repo. Actually maybe the `make install` in entire-agent-zed installs the entire CLI too? No, the Makefile only installs `entire-agent-zed`. \n\nWait, maybe the user means: the entire CLI (0.9.0) is installed, and the entire-agent-zed binary was installed via `make install`. Both are on PATH.\n\nOK here's my plan. Let me first verify the diagnosis thoroughly, then decide on the fix approach. Let me check:\n1. Does `entire agent add zed` fail? (reported yes)\n2. Does `entire agent list` show Zed? (reported no)\n3. Does `entire enable --agent zed` work? (reported \"appears to install the hooks\")\n\nLet me verify #3 and check what hooks it installs. Let me also check the `entire` binary's behavior with `--agent zed`.\n\nActually, I already saw the git hooks were installed (post-commit, commit-msg, etc.) at 22:28. And `are-hooks-installed` returns false because the zed agent's own hook marker isn't in the post-commit hook. The `entire enable --agent zed` installed the generic entire git hooks, NOT the zed-specific lifecycle hook.\n\nWait, this is important. Let me understand the difference:\n- `entire enable --agent zed` → calls `setupAgentHooksNonInteractive` → `setupAgentHooks` → `hookAgent.InstallHooks(ctx, localDev, forceHooks)`. For an external agent, `InstallHooks` calls the binary's `install-hooks` subcommand. So it SHOULD install the zed lifecycle hook (the \"Entire Zed Agent Lifecycle Hook\" block in post-commit).\n\nBut the current post-commit hook doesn't have the zed marker. Let me check again... The post-commit hook content is:\n```\n#!/bin/sh\n# Entire CLI hooks\n# Post-commit hook: condense session data if commit has Entire-Checkpoint trailer\nif command -v entire >/dev/null 2>&1; then entire hooks git post-commit 2>/dev/null || true; else :; fi\n# Chain: run pre-existing hook\n_entire_hook_dir=\"$(dirname \"$0\")\"\nif [ -x \"$_entire_hook_dir/post-commit.pre-entire\" ]; then\n    \"$_entire_hook_dir/post-commit.pre-entire\" \"$@\"\nfi\n```\n\nThis is the generic entire git hook, NOT the zed lifecycle hook. So `entire enable --agent zed` did NOT install the zed agent's hook. Hmm.\n\nWait, but the user said \"`entire enable --agent zed` appears to install the hooks\". Maybe it did install the generic hooks but not the zed-specific lifecycle hook. Let me check the `entire` log to see what happened.\n\nActually, let me check the entire log.",
