          "text": "Now I have a complete picture. Let me now implement the plugin compliance fixes. I'll add the following subcommands:\n\n1. `detect` → `{\"present\": true/false}` based on whether Zed DB exists\n2. `get-session-id` → read HookInput JSON from stdin, derive session ID, return `{\"session_id\": ...}`\n3. `read-session` → read HookInput JSON, return AgentSession JSON\n4. `write-session` → read AgentSession JSON from stdin, best-effort write, return `{}`\n5. `read-transcript --session-ref <path>` → return raw transcript bytes (from DB)\n6. `chunk-transcript --max-size <n>` → read raw bytes from stdin, split into base64 chunks\n7. `reassemble-transcript` → read `{\"chunks\": [...]}`, decode base64, concatenate, output raw bytes\n8. `format-resume-command --session-id <id>` → return `{\"command\": \"\"}` (Zed has no CLI resume)\n9. `get-transcript-position --path <path>` → return file size (or message count for DB path)\n10. `extract-modified-files --path <path> --offset <n>` → parse transcript, extract file paths from tool uses\n11. `extract-prompts --session-ref <path> --offset <n>` → parse transcript, extract user prompts from offset\n12. `extract-summary --session-ref <path>` → return thread summary\n13. `prepare-transcript --session-ref <path>` → no-op exit 0 (DB already exists)\n\nPlus update `handleInfo` to add `hook_names`.\n\nLet me also think about what \"chunk-transcript\" should do: The CLI passes content bytes on stdin and `--max-size`. It should split into base64-encoded chunks of at most max-size bytes each and return `{\"chunks\": [[base64], ...]}`. But wait — `ChunkResponse.Chunks [][]byte` marshals each []byte as base64. So I need to produce base64 strings. Actually in Go, `[][]byte` marshals as array of base64 strings. So I can encode: read stdin, split into chunks of maxSize, base64 each, and put into `[][]byte`.\n\n`reassemble-transcript`: reads `{\"chunks\": [...]}` JSON where chunks are base64-encoded. Decode each, concatenate, output raw bytes to stdout.\n\nNow for `read-transcript --session-ref <path>`: The session_ref is the DB path. I should read the DB and return the raw thread data (decompressed). The CLI then writes it as the transcript file and passes it to `calculate-tokens`. So `read-transcript` should return the raw decompressed JSON bytes of the latest thread.\n\nFor `extract-modified-files --path <path> --offset <n>`: parse the transcript from DB, iterate messages from offset, collect file paths from tool uses. Zed's tool use inputs have various fields. Common Zed tools: `open_file`, `write`, `edit`, `create_file`, `rename`, `move`, `delete`, `terminal`... For modified files, look for `path` or `new_path` fields in tool use input. Return `{\"files\": [...], \"current_position\": <last message index>}`.\n\nFor `extract-prompts --session-ref <path> --offset <n>`: parse transcript from DB, collect user message contents from index offset onward. Return `{\"prompts\": [...]}`.\n\nFor `extract-summary --session-ref <path>`: return the thread summary from the DB. Return `{\"summary\": ..., \"has_summary\": true/false}`.\n\nFor `get-transcript-position --path <path>`: return the current position in the transcript. Since the transcript is a DB, position could be the message count of the latest thread. Return `{\"position\": msgCount}`.\n\nFor `prepare-transcript --session-ref <path>`: The DB exists so no-op. Return `{}`.\n\nFor `format-resume-command --session-id <id>`: Zed has no CLI. Return `{\"command\": \"\"}`.\n\nFor `get-session-id`: read HookInput JSON, use session_id field if present else derive. Return `{\"session_id\": ...}`.\n\nFor `read-session`: read HookInput JSON, return AgentSession JSON with session_id, agent_name \"zed\", repo_path, session_ref (dbPath), native_data (raw thread data).\n\nFor `write-session`: read AgentSession JSON from stdin, best-effort write session snapshot, return `{}`.\n\nNow, the important design decision: the session_ref is the DB path. The CLI's `fileExists(transcriptRef)` check passes because the DB file exists. Then `ReadTranscript(transcriptRef)` calls `read-transcript --session-ref <dbPath>` which returns raw thread data. Then `ExtractPrompts(transcriptRef, offset)` → `extract-prompts --session-ref <dbPath>`. Then `ExtractModifiedFilesFromOffset(transcriptRef, offset)` → `extract-modified-files --path <dbPath>`. Then `CalculateTokenUsage(transcriptData, offset)` → `calculate-tokens` with transcriptData on stdin.\n\nSo the current `handleCalculateTokens` reads from DB but ignores stdin and offset. I should update it to read stdin first if provided (that's the actual transcript data), and respect the offset. Actually the current one ignores offset entirely. Let me make it: if stdin is non-empty, parse that; else fetch from DB. And apply offset if provided (extract delta from offset).\n\nHmm, but offset semantics for tokens: the CLI passes `fromOffset` which is `transcriptOffset` (message count watermark). The token calc is supposed to be for new messages since offset. But the raw thread's `request_token_usage` is per-request. Filtering by message index is complex. Given best-effort, I'll keep the token calc as-is (sum all request_token_usage), but read from stdin if provided. Actually, wait — let me keep it simpler: keep reading from the DB (the source of truth), since that's the current behavior and it produces valid TokenUsage. The stdin path is a bonus. Actually let me just prefer stdin when provided since that's the protocol contract.\n\nLet me now also handle the offset in extract-prompts/extract-modified-files. The offset is a message index (transcript_start). So slice messages from offset.\n\nLet me write the code. I'll add a helper to open the DB given a session-ref path (which may be the DB path or \"zed-db\"). Since getZedDBPath() already resolves the real path, I can just use it. For session-ref values that differ, best-effort: if the path exists and is the known DB path, use it; otherwise try opening as DB.\n\nLet me write a helper `openDBFromRef(sessionRef string)`:\n- If sessionRef != \"\" and sessionRef != \"zed-db\": try to open it read-only.\n- Else use getZedDBPath().\n\nActually simpler: always resolve via getZedDBPath() when sessionRef is empty or \"zed-db\"; otherwise try the given path.\n\nLet me now write all the new handlers and wire up the switch. Also add `hook_names` to info.\n\nLet me plan the code structure:\n\n```go\nfunc handleDetect() {\n\tpresent := false\n\tif _, err := getZedDBPath(); err == nil {\n\t\tpresent = true\n\t}\n\tout := map[string]interface{}{\"present\": present}\n\tjson.NewEncoder(os.Stdout).Encode(out)\n}\n```\n\n```go\nfunc handleGetSessionID() {\n\tsessionID := deriveSessionIDFromStdin()\n\tout := map[string]interface{}{\"session_id\": sessionID}\n\tjson.NewEncoder(os.Stdout).Encode(out)\n}\n```\n\nderiveSessionID: read stdin JSON (HookInput), if session_id present use it; else derive from latest thread.\n\n```go\nfunc handleReadSession() {\n\t// read HookInput JSON\n\tvar input map[string]interface{}\n\tjson.NewDecoder(os.Stdin).Decode(&input)\n\tsessionID := deriveSessionID()\n\tthreadID, _, _, rawData := getLatestThreadMeta()\n\tdbPath, _ := getZedDBPath()\n\trepoPath := repoRoot()\n\tout := map[string]interface{}{\n\t\t\"session_id\": sessionID,\n\t\t\"agent_name\": \"zed\",\n\t\t\"repo_path\":  repoPath,\n\t\t\"session_ref\": dbPath,\n\t\t\"start_time\": time.Now().Format(time.RFC3339),\n\t}\n\tif rawData != nil {\n\t\tout[\"native_data\"] = rawData\n\t}\n\tjson.NewEncoder(os.Stdout).Encode(out)\n}\n```\n\n```go\nfunc handleWriteSession() {\n\tvar sess map[string]interface{}\n\tjson.NewDecoder(os.Stdin).Decode(&sess)\n\t// best-effort: write to .git/entire-sessions/\n\tif sid, ok := sess[\"session_id\"].(string); ok && sid != \"\" {\n\t\t... write snapshot\n\t}\n\tfmt.Println(\"{}\")\n}\n```\n\n```go\nfunc handleReadTranscript() {\n\tsessionRef := flagValue(\"--session-ref\")\n\traw, err := readTranscriptFromRef(sessionRef)\n\tif err != nil {\n\t\tfmt.Println(\"{}\")\n\t\treturn\n\t}\n\tos.Stdout.Write(raw)\n}\n```\n\nreadTranscriptFromRef: open DB (from ref or default), fetch latest thread, return raw decompressed data.\n\n```go\nfunc handleChunkTranscript() {\n\tmaxSize := parseInt flag --max-size, default e.g. 1024*1024\n\tdata, _ := io.ReadAll(os.Stdin)\n\tvar chunks [][]byte\n\tfor len(data) > 0 {\n\t\tn := len(data)\n\t\tif n > maxSize { n = maxSize }\n\t\tchunks = append(chunks, data[:n])\n\t\tdata = data[n:]\n\t}\n\tout := map[string]interface{}{\"chunks\": chunks}\n\tjson.NewEncoder(os.Stdout).Encode(out)\n}\n```\n\nNote: base64 is handled automatically by encoding/json for [][]byte.\n\n```go\nfunc handleReassembleTranscript() {\n\tvar req struct{ Chunks [][]byte `json:\"chunks\"` }\n\tjson.NewDecoder(os.Stdin).Decode(&req)\n\tvar out bytes.Buffer\n\tfor _, c := range req.Chunks {\n\t\tout.Write(c)\n\t}\n\tos.Stdout.Write(out.Bytes())\n}\n```\n\n```go\nfunc handleFormatResumeCommand() {\n\t// Zed has no CLI resume; return empty command\n\tout := map[string]interface{}{\"command\": \"\"}\n\tjson.NewEncoder(os.Stdout).Encode(out)\n}\n```\n\n```go\nfunc handleGetTranscriptPosition() {\n\tpath := flagValue(\"--path\")\n\t// position = message count of latest thread in DB at path\n\t... return {\"position\": msgCount}\n}\n```\n\n```go\nfunc handleExtractModifiedFiles() {\n\tpath := flagValue(\"--path\")\n\toffset := flagInt(\"--offset\")\n\traw := readTranscriptFromPath(path)\n\ttranscript, _ := parseTranscript(raw)\n\tvar files []string\n\tseen := map[string]bool{}\n\tcurrentPos := offset\n\tfor i := offset; i < len(transcript); i++ {\n\t\tcurrentPos = i\n\t\tfor _, tool := range transcript[i].Tools {\n\t\t\tfor _, f := range filePathsFromTool(tool) {\n\t\t\t\tif !seen[f] { seen[f] = true; files = append(files, f) }\n\t\t\t}\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\tout := map[string]interface{}{\"files\": files, \"current_position\": currentPos}\n\tjson.NewEncoder(os.Stdout).Encode(out)\n}\n```\n\nfilePathsFromTool: inspect tool.Params for \"path\" or \"new_path\" string values. Zed tools like `open_file`, `write`, `edit`, `create_file`... Actually modified files would come from write/edit/create/rename/move/delete tools. Let me just collect any string value under keys \"path\", \"new_path\", \"old_path\", \"file_path\", \"filePath\", \"name\"? Hmm. To be safe, collect \"path\" and \"new_path\" keys. And tool names containing \"write\", \"edit\", \"create\", \"rename\", \"move\", \"delete\", \"remove\", \"mkdir\", \"open\" — open_file reads but might also be considered. Let me only include mutating tools. Actually for the CLI's checkpoint, the modified files list is used to track which files changed. Since git status is the primary detection, this is a supplement. I'll include tools whose name (lowercased) contains any of: \"write\", \"edit\", \"create\", \"rename\", \"move\", \"delete\", \"remove\", \"mkdir\", \"overwrite\", \"append\". If tool name is generic/unknown, fall back to including any \"path\" field? No — safer to only include known mutating tools plus generic tools with path when name contains \"file\". Let me keep it simple: include path values from tool inputs for tools matching the mutating keyword set; otherwise skip.\n\nActually, to be practical and useful, I'll extract from any tool use that has a string \"path\"/\"new_path\" in its input, EXCEPT read-only tools (open_file, read, search, grep, list, get_text, find, view). This yields modified files.\n\n```go\nfunc handleExtractPrompts() {\n\tref := flagValue(\"--session-ref\")\n\toffset := flagInt(\"--offset\")\n\traw := readTranscriptFromRef(ref)\n\ttranscript, _ := parseTranscript(raw)\n\tvar prompts []string\n\tfor i := offset; i < len(transcript); i++ {\n\t\tif transcript[i].Role == \"user\" && strings.TrimSpace(transcript[i].Content) != \"\" {\n\t\t\tprompts = append(prompts, transcript[i].Content)\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\tout := map[string]interface{}{\"prompts\": prompts}\n\tjson.NewEncoder(os.Stdout).Encode(out)\n}\n```\n\n```go\nfunc handleExtractSummary() {\n\tref := flagValue(\"--session-ref\")\n\tdbPath := resolveDBRef(ref)\n\tdb, _ := sql.Open(\"sqlite3\", dbPath+\"?mode=ro\")\n\tt, _, err := fetchLatestThread(db)\n\tsummary := \"\"\n\thas := false\n\tif err == nil {\n\t\tsummary = t.Summary\n\t\thas = summary != \"\"\n\t}\n\tout := map[string]interface{}{\"summary\": summary, \"has_summary\": has}\n\tjson.NewEncoder(os.Stdout).Encode(out)\n}\n```\n\n```go\nfunc handlePrepareTranscript() {\n\t// DB already materialized; no-op\n\tfmt.Println(\"{}\")\n}\n```\n\nNow the helpers:\n- `flagValue(name)` — parse os.Args for `--name value`.\n- `flagInt(name)` — parse int.\n- `resolveDBRef(ref)` — if ref != \"\" && ref != \"zed-db\", return ref; else getZedDBPath().\n- `readTranscriptFromRef(ref)` — open DB, fetch latest, return raw.\n- `repoRoot()` — ENTIRE_REPO_ROOT or git.\n\nAlso update info to add hook_names: `[\"session-start\", \"turn-start\", \"turn-end\", \"session-end\"]`.\n\nLet me also make handleCalculateTokens prefer stdin when provided. Actually, given the CLI calls it with transcriptData on stdin, and the current handler reads from DB, it works but ignores the offset. Since the raw data on stdin is the same latest thread, results are equivalent. I'll update it to read stdin when present.\n\nLet me now also double check: for `extract-modified-files`, the CLI also has a subagent-aware extractor check. Since the plugin doesn't declare subagent_aware_extractor capability, it uses the basic path. Good.\n\nAlso, `calculate-tokens --offset <n>`: the CLI passes transcriptData (raw bytes) on stdin. I'll update handleCalculateTokens to read stdin if non-empty.\n\nOne more consideration: `read-transcript` returns raw bytes (not JSON). The CLI writes them to the transcript file and later passes them to calculate-tokens. Good.\n\nNow let me write the code. I'll add the new handlers after handleResolveSessionFile (around line 1594) and update the switch and info.\n\nLet me also add flag parsing helpers near the top or bottom.\n\nLet me write the edits now. First, update the switch statement.",
