ifiedFilesFromOffset(path, startOffset)` → `--path <path> --offset <n>`. Parse the transcript (from DB at path), extract file paths from tool uses, return `{\"files\": [...], \"current_position\": <msgCount>}`.\n\nFor Zed, tool uses have names like \"open_file\", \"write\", \"edit\", \"create_file\", \"rename\", \"move\", \"delete\", \"terminal\", \"bash\". Modified files would be those referenced in tool use inputs. Zed's tool input typically has a \"path\" field. Let me extract \"path\" and \"new_path\" fields from tool use inputs for mutating tools. Actually, to be safe, extract any \"path\" string field from tool use inputs. That's the best-effort approach.\n\n**extract-prompts**: CLI calls `ExtractPrompts(sessionRef, fromOffset)` → `--session-ref <path> --offset <n>`. Parse transcript, extract user message contents from index >= offset. Return `{\"prompts\": [...]}`.\n\n**extract-summary**: CLI calls `ExtractSummary(sessionRef)` → `--session-ref <path>`. Return thread summary from DB. Return `{\"summary\": ..., \"has_summary\": bool}`.\n\n**prepare-transcript**: CLI calls `PrepareTranscript(ctx, sessionRef)` → `--session-ref <path>`. Since the DB always exists, no-op. Return `{}`.\n\nNow let me write these handlers. I'll add them after `handleResolveSessionFile` (around line 1594).\n\nLet me also think about the `read-transcript` returning raw bytes. The CLI writes it to the transcript file. Then `ExtractPrompts(sessionRef, offset)` is called with the sessionRef (DB path), not the transcript file. So extract-prompts must open the DB. OK.\n\nLet me write a helper to open the DB from a session-ref path:\n```go\nfunc openDBFromRef(sessionRef string) (*sql.DB, string, error) {\n    dbPath := sessionRef\n    if dbPath == \"\" || dbPath == \"zed-db\" {\n        p, err := getZedDBPath()\n        if err != nil {\n            return nil, \"\", err\n        }\n        dbPath = p\n    }\n    db, err := sql.Open(\"sqlite3\", dbPath+\"?mode=ro\")\n    if err != nil {\n     