          "text": "You're right — this repo's active agent is opencode (the `ses_0209a89b9ffezrYHoP3rdb5iwF` session in the logs), so the commit I made fired the opencode lifecycle, not zed. The zed block in `post-commit.pre-entire` is vestigial from an earlier `entire enable --agent zed` run and only fires if a Zed session is active — which isn't the case here. Testing zed via git commits in this repo is measuring the wrong thing.\n\nWhat we've actually established for the zed plugin:\n\n1. **Protocol compliance fixed** — all subcommands implemented, `hook_names` present, build + tests green.\n2. **`entire hooks zed <event>` works** — the discovery path that uses `detect` fires zed lifecycle events correctly (session-start/turn-start/turn-end all logged, checkpoint saved).\n3. **`entire agent add/list` still fails** — `Unknown agent \"zed\"`; this is the CLI-side discovery gap (noun-group never calls external discovery), confirmed unchanged.\n\nSo per your plan, the plugin is compliant and functional; the remaining blocker is the CLI's `agent add/list` discovery — which becomes a filed bug issue, not a local patch.\n\nWhere do you want to go from here? Options: (a) file the Entire bug issue for `agent add/list` discovery, (b) write the hook-testing runbook, or (c) something else.",
